@page "/docs/components/toc"
@using Blaizio.Docs.Examples.TableOfContents
<PageTitle>Table of Contents - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="typeset typeset-docs space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Table of Contents</h1>
            <DocBadges Slug="toc" />
        </div>
        <p>An "On this page" navigation built from the headings it finds in a region of your page, with the section in view highlighted as you scroll. Headings without an id get one, and a click on a link scrolls smoothly to its section. Beyond a plain list it comes as a rail, a progress line or a minimap of tick marks, and the right rail of this site is one.</p>
    </header>

    <UsageBlock Example="typeof(TableOfContentsUsageExample)"
                Description="Point `ContentSelector` at the region to scan and `RootSelector` at its scroll container. The navigation lists the headings and tracks the one in view." />

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Peek wave</h2>
        <p>Set <code>Wave</code> on the Peek variant for a dock-style magnification. The line under the pointer grows the longest, its neighbors a step less and the next pair less still, and the swell follows the pointer smoothly down the column. Tabbing through the links plays the same wave, and each line still opens its own label. The lines sit closer together than in plain Peek, which leaves room for the swell.</p>
        <p>A tick's length shows its heading level, so sections that all sit at one level (a step list, a timeline, a changelog) draw an even column of equal lines, and the swell stands out most clearly against that. Since the ticks carry no text, <code>PeekContent</code> supplies what each label shows.</p>
        <p>Two parameters change the geometry. <code>MarkWidth</code> sets the resting length of a section line on any minimap variant, and every level derives from it. <code>WaveAmplitude</code> sets how far the hovered line grows.</p>
    </section>

    <ExamplesHeading />

    <Demo Title="Default"
          Description="Set `RootSelector` to a scrollable box and the navigation tracks that box instead of the page. Scroll the article to see the active heading follow, or click a link to jump."
          Example="typeof(TableOfContentsDefaultExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Variants"
          Description="`List` is the plain set of links, and `Rail` adds a hairline down the start edge with one indicator that slides between sections. `Progress` fills the line as the region scrolls. `Marks` is a minimap of dashes that reveals all its labels on hover or keyboard focus, while `Peek`, shown in its own demos below, opens one label at a time."
          Example="typeof(TableOfContentsVariantsExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Marks, floating"
          Description="The `Marks` minimap pinned to the start edge and centered. Hover it or Tab into it and the labels slide out one after another. Passing `absolute` in `Class` positions it within this box instead of the viewport."
          Example="typeof(TableOfContentsMarksExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Peek, wave"
          Description="`Wave` magnifies the lines around the pointer or keyboard focus as it moves down the column. These sections all sit at one level, so the column starts even, and `PeekContent` gives each card a step name, a description and a line of metadata."
          Example="typeof(TableOfContentsWaveExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Peek"
          Description="Only the ticks show, with no panel or title, and hovering or tabbing to one opens that heading's card alone. Ticks are long for `h2` and shorter for what nests under it, so the column sketches the shape of the page. `PeekContent` replaces the card's body, here with a heading and a line of description."
          Example="typeof(TableOfContentsPeekExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Position"
          Description="`Position` floats the panel at one of six anchors: start or end, at the top, center or bottom. The edges are logical, so a start panel sits on the left in a left-to-right page and on the right in a right-to-left one. `Inline`, the default, leaves the panel in the layout flow."
          Example="typeof(TableOfContentsPositionExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Top-level only"
          Description="Narrow `HeadingSelector` to skip sub-headings, and set your own title."
          Example="typeof(TableOfContentsFlatExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Customization"
          Description="`LinkClass` is merged over every link's defaults, so `data-active:` utilities style the current section, here with an accent bar and a tint. The panel has no surface of its own, so `Class` turns it into a card and sets its width, while `Sticky` keeps it pinned as you scroll."
          Example="typeof(TableOfContentsCustomExample)" />

    <Demo Title="RTL"
          Description="Inside a right-to-left `DirectionProvider`, the rail and its active marker move to the right edge and the links align to it."
          Example="typeof(TableOfContentsRtlExample)" />
</div>

